  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThe second half of the week is going to remain stormy. A ridge \nover the Southeast and a slow-moving shortwave trough over the \nwestern CONUS is forecast to pull multiple, weak subtropical \ntroughs northeastward into the Midwest. With the stationary \nfront from Tuesday's system acting as the source of lift, \nscattered showers and storms will likely develop daily. With \nsaturated grounds expected after the rounds of rain today \nthrough Tuesday, the flood threat will become more elevated with\neach subsequent round. The WPC already has a Slight Risk, Level\n2 out of 4, for excessive rainfall Monday night into Tuesday, \nand another areawide Slight Risk for Wednesday. In terms of \nrainfall totals, latest LREF guidance has a 60-80% of 2+ inches \nacross eastern Iowa and 30-50% across northwest Illinois and \nnortheast Missouri through Saturday. There is a lot of \nuncertainty though in the axis (or axes) of heaviest rainfall \nwith the LREF 10th-90th percentile spread showing a 2-3 inch \ndifference areawide. Therefore, while the probabilities are low \n(10-20%) for 5+ inches of rainfall across eastern Iowa, the \nensemble spread is suggesting that latest model guidance may be \nthe undercutting the high-end totals.",
